# Roadmap — the single shared forward register

The **one** place a shared or cross-domain effort is named and tracked. A development *vertical to
one domain* stays in that domain's memory; this register holds only what spans domains or outlives a
session. Curated by the steward; **the lead proposes and flips their own rows.**

> ⚠️ **The table shape below is LOAD-BEARING.** `/crit-status-check` axis 3 (the `[SHARED]` tier of every
> agent's plan) and `/crit-doc-lint [6]` (STALE-Rx) both parse it **by column position**. Keep four columns
> in this order — `Rx | What | Lead | Status` — or an agent's own roadmap items stop appearing in
> their plan.

## Rules

- **`Rx` cell holds the id alone** (`R7`), nothing else. A struck row (`~~R7~~`) is skipped.
- **`Lead` names the owning agent(s)** as `A<n>` or `AGENT <n>` (`A5 + A1` for a shared lead) — this
  is matched against the agent asking "what's on my plan".
- **`Status` starts with its verdict word**, because the parsers read the *first* word of that cell:
  `open …` · `✅ done …` · `⏸ parked …` · `⏸ deferred …` · `closed …` · `superseded …`.
  A status may *mention* a settled leg while being open (`open (L1 shipped · L2 deferred)`) — that is
  why the verdict must come **first** and why the parsers anchor on it.
- **The row body links, it does not restate.** A row is a *pointer* with enough context to know what
  it is: the design lives in the owning domain's `DECISIONS.md` / a design doc, cited by ID. A row
  that grows into a full design is a copy that will drift from its source.
- **Closure is the lead's, in the same round** the work lands (`contract-shared § 2`) — with proof, or as a
  one-line flip proposal to the steward. A landed effort whose row stays open re-litigates as "still
  to do".
- **Re-verifying a row touches it**, which resets its staleness clock: add
  `⟳ re-verified YYYY-MM-DD` to the Status cell when you confirm a row is genuinely still open.

